# Findings gitleaks should stop reporting, one fingerprint per line.
#
# This file exists because gitleaks scans HISTORY rather than the working tree: CI checks it out
# with `fetch-depth: 0` precisely so it can. A marker removed from a file today is still in the
# commit that added it, so it fails every run afterwards, and the only other ways out are
# rewriting published history or turning the check off. Neither is worth it for a test fixture.
#
# Nothing here is a real credential, and nothing should be added here that might be. A fingerprint
# is `commit:path:rule:line`, so each line pardons one finding in one commit rather than a file or
# a rule, and a genuine leak added later still fails.
#
# ---
#
# A test fixture for the GitHub App work: a deliberately broken PEM, written out to prove that an
# unparseable key is refused with a message naming the setting rather than accepted and failing
# later. It was never a key, only the header of one. The fixtures were rewritten afterwards to be
# cut from a key generated at runtime, so the marker is not in the tree any more, but it is still
# in the commit below.
